<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-algebra-in-other-activity">

	<title>Module 7: Algebra in Other Number Systems</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Into the Wild</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Power Sets</title>
		
		<definition>
			<statement><p>
			 Let <m>S</m> be a set.
  The <term>power set</term> of <m>S</m> denoted <m>\mathcal{P}(S)</m> is the
  collection of all subsets of <m>S</m>.
  That is <m>\mathcal{P}(S)=\{T: T\subseteq S\}</m>.
 			</p></statement>
		</definition>

		<definition>
			<statement><p>
				For any sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, the <term>symmetric difference</term> of
  <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, denoted <m>A \triangle B</m> is the set of all elements
  that belong to either <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>, but not both.
  That is,
  <md>
    A \triangle B = \{x: x\in A\cup B \text{ and }x\not\in A\cap B\}.
  </md>

			</p></statement>
		</definition>
		
		<definition>
			<statement><p>
				  For any natural number <m>n</m>, the number system <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m> is
  <m>(\mathcal{P}(\{1,2,\dots, n\}), \triangle, \cap)</m>.
  That is, the elements are the sets that are subsets of
  <m>\{1,2,\dots, n\}</m>, and for <m>A,B\in \mathcal{P}_{n}</m> we define
  <m>A+B = A\triangle B</m> and <m>A\cdot B = A \cap B</m>.
			</p></statement>
		</definition>
    </paragraphs>
    
    <paragraphs>
    	<title>Working with <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m></title>
	
		<exercise workspace="1in">
			<p>Write the addition and multiplication tables for <m>\mathcal{P}_{2}</m>.</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
			<p>Is there an additive identity in <m>\mathcal{P}_{2}</m>? What about a multiplicative identity in <m>\mathcal{P}_{2}</m>? Do these generalize to <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m>?</p>
		</exercise>
	</paragraphs>
    </page>

	<page>
	
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
			<p>Does every element of <m>\mathcal{P}_{2}</m> have an additive inverse? What elements of <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m> do you think have an additive inverse?</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
			<p>Is every nonzero element of <m>\mathcal{P}_{2}</m> a unit? What elements of <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m> do you think are units?</p>
		</exercise>

		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
			<p>Does <m>\mathcal{P}_{2}</m> have zero divisors? Do you think <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m> has zero divisors?</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
			<p>Do you think <m>\mathcal{P}_{n}</m> is a ring? A commutative ring? A ring with identity? An integral domain? A field?</p>
		</exercise>
	</page>
	
	<page>
		<paragraphs>
		<title>Polynomials</title>
		
		<definition>
			<statement>
			<p>
  Let <m>R</m> be a commutative ring.
  A <term>polynomial in <m>x</m> over <m>R</m> </term> is an expression of the form
  <md>
    a_{n}x^{n} + a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+ \cdots a_{2}x^{2} + a_{1}x^{1} + a_{0}x^{0},
  </md>
  where <m>n</m> is a nonnegative integer and
  <m>a_{n},a_{n-1},\dots,a_{2},a_{1},a_{0}</m> are elements of <m>R</m>.
  The <em>polynomial ring over <m>R</m> </em> is the set of all polynomials over
  the ring <m>R</m>, and is denoted <m>R[x]</m>.
  (Note this language implies the polynomials form a ring.
  We'll take this as true, and just verify a couple of properties.
  The proof is mostly tedious and not insightful.
</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		
		<definition>
			<statement>
			<p>
  <term>Adding Polynomials</term>: The <term>sum of polynomials</term> <m>p(x)</m> and <m>q(x)</m> is defined to be
  <md>
    p(x) + q(x) = (a_{n}+b_{n})x^{n} + (a_{n-1}+b_{n-1})x^{n-1}+ \cdots + (a_{1}+b_{1}) x + (a_{0}+b_{0}).
  </md>
</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		
		<definition>
			<statement>
			<p>
  <term>Multiplying Polynomials</term>: The
  <term>product of polynomials</term> <m>p(x)</m> (of degree <m>m</m>) and
  <m>q(x)</m> (of degree <m>n</m>) is defined to be
  <md>
    p(x)q(x) = c_{m+n}x^{m+n}+ c_{m+n-1}x^{m+n-1}+ \cdots + c_{2}x^{2} + c_{1}x + c_{0}.
  </md>
  where for each <m>k</m> with <m>0\le k \le m+n</m>,
  <md>
    c_{k} = a_{k}b_{0} + a_{k-1}b_{1} + a_{k-2}b_{2} + \cdots + a_{2}b_{k-2}+ a_{1}b_{k-1}+a_{0}b_{k}.
  </md>
</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
		<p>
  Add and multiply <m>p(x) = 2x^{2}+4x+2</m> and <m>q(x)=3x+3</m> using the
  definitions above.
  Does this agree with what you think the sum and product should be?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
		<p>
  <em>If time:</em> Let <m>r(x)= 5x+4</m>.
  Find <m>p(x)(q(x)+r(x))</m> and <m>p(x)q(x)+p(x)r(x)</m>.
  What property of rings does this illustrate?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
		<p>
  Is <m>\mathbb{Z}[x]</m> a commutative ring with identity?
  Is <m>\mathbb{E}[x]</m> a commutative ring with identity?
  Is <m>R[x]</m> always, sometimes or never a commutative ring?
  A ring with identity?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
		<p>
  Let <m>f(x)=[2]x^{2} + [4]x + [2]</m> and <m>g(x) = [3]x+[3]</m> be
  polynomials in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}[x]</m>.
  Find <m>f(x)g(x)</m>.
  Is <m>R[x]</m> always, sometimes, or never an integral domain?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
		<p>
  At some point you (maybe) learned that the if <m>p(x)</m> has degree <m>m</m>
  and <m>q(x)</m> has degree <m>n</m> then the degree of <m>p(x)q(x)</m> is
  <m>m+n</m>.
  There, you were thinking about <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>.
  Do the degrees work out this way in <m>R[x]</m> for any commutative ring
  <m>R</m>?
  If not always, when will it work?
  To explain, determine what the <q>leading coefficient</q> of <m>p(x)q(x)</m> is
  for arbitrary polynomials <m>p(x)</m> and <m>q(x)</m>.
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
		<p>
  If <m>R</m> has an identity, the multiplicative identity of <m>R[x]</m> is
  <m>i(x) = 1</m>.
  What elements of <m>R[x]</m> will have multiplicative inverses?
		</p>
		</exercise>
	</page>

<page xml:id="sec-alegbra-in-other-review">

<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
Make sure you can answer the following questions:

<ul>
	<li><p>What axioms hold in a ring?</p></li>
	<li><p>What is <m>\mathcal{P}_n</m> and is it a ring, an integral domain, a field?</p></li>
	<li><p>What is <m>R[x]</m> for a commutative ring <m>R</m> and is it a ring, an integral domain, a field?</p></li>
</ul>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 7</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<title>Positive reals</title>
		<introduction><p>
    Let <m>\mathbb{R}^{+}</m> denote the set of all positive real numbers.
    (Note <m>0</m> is not positive!) For all <m>x,y\in \mathbb{R}^{+}</m> define
    <md>
        x\oplus y = xy \text{ and }x\otimes y = x^{\log(y)}.
    </md>

		</p></introduction>
		
		<task><p>With these properties, does <m>\mathbb{R}^{+}</m> have an additive identity?
    If so, what is it?</p></task>
		<task><p>With these properties, does <m>\mathbb{R}^{+}</m> have a multiplicative
    identity?
    If so, what is it?</p></task>
		<task><p>Is <m>\mathbb{R}^{+}</m> a ring with these operations?
    Explain each axiom.</p></task>
		<task><p>Does <m>\mathbb{R}^{+}</m> have zero divisors using these operations?</p></task>
		<task><p>Is <m>\mathbb{R}^{+}</m> an integral domain using these operations?</p></task>
    	</exercise>
	

		<exercise number="2">
		<title>Complex numbers</title>
		<introduction><p>A <term>complex number</term> is any number of the form <m>a+bi</m> where
    <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>i</m> is an imaginary number with the
    property that <m>i^{2} = -1</m>.
    For a complex number <m>x=a+bi</m>, the real number is called the
    <term>real part</term> of <m>x</m> and <m>b</m> is called the
    <term>imaginary part</term> of <m>x</m>.
    So
    <md>
        \mathbb{C}= \{a+bi: a\in \mathbb{R}, b\in\mathbb{R}, \text{ and }i^{2} = -1\}.
    </md>
    Furthermore, addition and multiplication within <m>\mathbb{C}</m> are
    defined by
    <md>
        (a+bi) + (c+di) = (a+c) + (b+d)i
    </md>
    and
    <md>
        (a+bi)(c+di) = (ac - bd) + (ad+bc)i.
    </md>
    The complex numbers turn out to be a field.
    Let's show every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse.
    That is, we want to show, for <m>x=a+bi</m> where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are
    not both <m>0</m>, there exists <m>y=c+di</m> such that
    <md>
        1 + 0i = xy = (a+bi)\cdot (c+di) = (ac-bd) +(ad+bc)i.
    </md>
	</p>

	<p>
    So we want <m>ac-bd = 1</m> and <m>ad+bc = 0</m>.
	</p>
		</introduction>

        	<task><p>
    Solve the system of equations
</p>
<md>
    <mrow>ac-bd&amp;= 1</mrow>
    <mrow>ad+bc&amp;= 0</mrow>
</md>
<p>
    for <m>c</m> and <m>d</m>, getting expressions that only depend on <m>a</m>
    and <m>b</m>.
    (You can do so using substitution.
    One way is to solve for <m>d</m> in the second equation, plug your answer
    into the first equation, and then solve for <m>c</m>.
    Then use what you know for <m>c</m> to find <m>d</m>.)
</p>
</task>
            <task><p>Verify that your
    answer in (a) gives the multiplicative inverse for <m>a+bi</m>.
    That is, <m>(a+bi)(c+di) = 1 + 0i</m>.</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
	</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>

		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Examples or not?</title>
		<introduction><p>Give an example of each of the following or explain why no such example exists.
		</p></introduction>
		
		<task><p>Two polynomials of degree <m>1</m> in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{4}[x]</m> whose product
    is a polynomial of degree <m>1</m>.</p></task>
		<task><p>Two polynomials of degree <m>3</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m> whose sum is a
    polynomial of degree <m>1</m>.</p></task>
		<task><p>Two polynomials of degree <m>2</m> in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{4}[x]</m> whose product
    is a polynomial of degree <m>5</m>.</p></task>
		</exercise>
	
	
 </page>
 
 </worksheet>
 
